# Tracking

Progress lives in **one place per thing that ships**: a file under
`doc/status/`, one for the language, one for each tool, and one for the site.
There is no repository-wide task list. Two overlapping trackers were tried
twice — a repo-root index of every work point, and a tracker beside each
proposal — and both failed the same way: a task could hide in either, and
neither was wrong to omit it.

A tool owns its own state, because a tool is what a reader wants the state of. A
proposal is an argument, and one argument is often settled across two tools
while another tool is settled by three proposals at once.

## Decision versus state

Two kinds of file, and the split between them is the whole convention:

| File | Answers | Changes when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `doc/proposals/HMD-NNNN/README.md` | What was decided, and why | The design changes |
| `doc/status/<tool>.md` | How far the code has got | The commits change |

A proposal carries no tracker of its own. Its rows are spread across whichever
trackers own the code it touches, each citing it, and the proposal index says
which ones those are.

A record that carries a to-do list stops being a record, because every reader
then has to work out which sentences are still true. Move the work point to the
tracker and let the record describe the decision as though it were finished.

## What a tracker holds

**Done** first, so the file opens with what exists rather than what does not.
Then a TODO split four ways, and the split matters more than it looks:

- **Planned work** — specified, unblocked, not started.
- **Broken** — known defects. A row leaves this table only when a gate would
  catch the defect's return, not when someone believes it is fixed.
- **Limitations** — known, accepted, not being fixed now. Each carries *why it
  stands*, which is what stops it being rediscovered as a bug.
- **Open questions and blockers** — what must be resolved before the proposal
  behind the row moves from `drafted` to `accepted`, and anything else genuinely
  undecided.

Every row carries a **state** — `done`, `ready`, `blocked`, `parked`, `open`,
`deferred`, `standing` — because a table of rows that all look alike is a list,
not a tracker. The reader must be able to see which rows are moving.

A defect and a limitation look identical in a bug report and are opposites in a
tracker: one is a promise to fix, the other is a decision not to. Keeping them
in separate tables forces the author to say which one they mean.

Most trackers end with the gates that verify their Done rows, and a changelog of
tracker edits — dated lines, newest last.

## The rule that makes it work

**Update the tracker in the same commit that changes the code.** A tracker
updated afterwards describes a repository that no longer exists, and one updated
in advance describes a repository that does not exist yet.

Trackers are also the one place in this knowledge base where identifiers may
carry the meaning — `M4.2`, `L6`, `Q3`. A tracker is a table of IDs by nature,
and its rows are read one at a time rather than as prose. Everywhere else,
including this card, a claim is restated rather than cited.

## Where progress may not live

Not in `doc/memory/`, which holds small real-time decisions and no state. Not in
a wiki card, including this one. Not in a proposal's own `README.md`. Not in a
tool's `README.md`, which is a page for the people who use it rather than a
place to keep a list of what it cannot do yet. A decision that needs discussion
becomes an **open question** in the tracker and is argued wherever it belongs.

Work that belongs to no tool and to no proposal has nowhere to live — which is
usually the signal that a decision has not been taken yet, rather than that the
tracker is missing a row.

## The board is not the tracker

[[kanban]] indexes the numbered issues under `doc/issues/`: work currently in
flight, one flat file each. The tracker answers *how far this tool has got*; the
board answers *what is being worked on right now*. An issue is usually the
short-lived thing, and the tracker row it moves is the durable one — a card
picked up becomes a row, and the card stops being the record of it.

See [[hmd-feature-list]] for the same distinction applied to the format itself:
that card tracks what the *format* has, a tracker tracks what one
*implementation* has, and they answer different questions.

## Place in the knowledge base

The models under `doc/models/` declare what the system is; proposals record
decisions; the board carries work in flight; trackers say how far each tool has
implemented them. See [[hypermarkdown]] for the card format this convention
is written in.
